// The standard library: the components an application is built from, the
// event payloads they emit, and the supporting types -- `Style`, the style
// enums, `Alert`, `File`, `Test`, `error`, and the `i18n` and `html`
// namespaces.
//
// Nothing here is in scope until it is imported. `import . "sngl:std"`
// writes its declarations unqualified; `import <alias> "sngl:std"` qualifies
// them, and is also how a platform overrides a component (`component
// <alias>.vbox`).
//
// It also carries the `#[foreign]` mark, which records what a declaration
// corresponds to outside SNGL: the import path it comes from and its name
// there -- `#[foreign("js:example.com/api", "Entry")]` -- or, given one
// argument, the name alone, which is all a struct field can say
// (`#[foreign("Title")]`). A struct, a struct field and a function may carry
// it. It imports nothing, is checked against nothing, and confers no type
// identity, since several SNGL types can map to one target-language type.
//
// The declaration stays the program's own: the mark says how to spell it for
// the language it names, and a backend generating another language ignores it
// rather than emitting a name that language has never heard of.
//
// A function's mark may add flags: `#[foreign("js:./api", "add", pure)]`.
// They state what a call costs, which is what SNGL cannot read off a body that
// describes the foreign declaration rather than implementing it. `async` says
// the declaration returns a promise its caller awaits. `pure` is not metadata
// -- it lets the compiler evaluate a call at build time, so a function wrongly
// marked pure is run during the build.
